  <static seed="686" source="Library/Michigan/Chap18Sec3/Q15.pg">
    <statement><p>Use the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals to calculate
<m>\int_C\vec F\cdot d\vec r</m> exactly, if
<m>\vec F = 5 x^{2/5}\,\vec i + e^{y/7}\,\vec j</m>, and <m>C</m> is the
quarter of the unit circle in the first quadrant, traced
counterclockwise from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(0,1)</m>.</p>
<p><m>\int_C\vec F\cdot d\vec r =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-2.49647</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\vec F = 5 x^{2/5}\,\vec i + e^{y/7}\,\vec j =
    \nabla\left(\frac{25}{7} x^{7/5} + 7 e^{y/7}\right)</m>, we have
<me>\int_C(5 x^{2/5}\,\vec i+e^{y/7}\,\vec j)\cdot d\vec r =
    \frac{25}{7} x^{7/5} + 7 e^{y/7}\bigg|_{(1,0)}^{(0,1)} =
    7 e^{1/7} - \frac{25}{7} - 7 = 7 e^{1/7} - \frac{74}{7}.</me>



</solution>
